WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.667
A popular character, some serious legal&nbsp;
threats, and a glossy Netflix reboot. 

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:08.280
Here's&nbsp;the real reason the "Adult Wednesday&nbsp;
Addams" series all but disappeared.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.180
Netflix recently premiered a new trailer&nbsp;
for "Wednesday," a TV series produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.320
and directed by Tim Burton. As its title&nbsp;
suggests, "Wednesday Addams" will focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.326
Gomez and Morticia's teenage daughter Wednesday,&nbsp;
portrayed by Jenna Ortega, as its protagonist. 

00:00:21.326 --> 00:00:25.440
But&nbsp;Netflix's new series is far from the first&nbsp;
time the character has been expanded upon.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.160
Originating in the "Addams Family" comic&nbsp;
strip from cartoonist Charles Addams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.460
just like the rest of the Addams Family,&nbsp;
Wednesday Addams has already gone through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.000
numerous incarnations. Lisa Loring was&nbsp;
the first actor to portray Wednesday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.621
in the 1960s sitcom that marked the first of&nbsp;
many adaptations of its core cast of characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:45.300
Perhaps the most notable actor to appear on-screen&nbsp;
as Wednesday Addams is Christina Ricci, who played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.340
the character in the 1991 "The Addams Family"&nbsp;
film, and its 1993 sequel, "Addams Family Values."

00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:54.420
Some viewers might associate the character more&nbsp;
closely with writer and performer Melissa Hunter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.500
whose recent writing credits include series&nbsp;
like "Santa Clarita Diet" and "She-Hulk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.020
Attorney at Law." Prior to&nbsp;
her work on those two shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.040
Hunter created and starred in a YouTube&nbsp;
web series titled "Adult Wednesday Addams,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.580
consisting of short scenes of the iconic&nbsp;
"Addams Family" character all grown up.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.080
At the moment, however, "Adult Wednesday Addams"&nbsp;
is no longer available on Hunter's YouTube channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.460
where it originated. And in an interview with&nbsp;
Buzzfeed, "Adult Wednesday Addams" creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.260
Melissa Hunter finally revealed what happened to&nbsp;
her YouTube series. As it turns out, Hunter was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.220
compelled to remove the series from her channel&nbsp;
after receiving a cease and desist letter from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.960
Tee &amp; Charles Addams Foundation, which owns the&nbsp;
"Addams Family" copyright. Hunter told Buzzfeed,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.244
"But, the good news is, I still have the dress."

00:01:43.726 --> 00:01:45.960
Understandably, Hunter has&nbsp;
been reluctant to pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.119
any avenue that results in legal&nbsp;
action, and explained to Buzzfeed,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.580
While Hunter's web series became the target of a&nbsp;
copyright claim, an adult film parody based on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.294
work remains online, as do unofficially-uploaded&nbsp;
videos of "Adult Wednesday Addams" episodes.

00:02:07.294 --> 00:02:10.741
In an interview with Mike Elder&nbsp;
on his YouTube series Box Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.741 --> 00:02:14.880
Hunter described Wednesday Addams as a part&nbsp;
of her childhood identity. Hunter also said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.600
that "Adult Wednesday Addams" was a project she&nbsp;
spearheaded in order to put something out into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.740
the world that was entirely hers. Though&nbsp;
it may be over, past episodes of Hunter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.700
passion project are hosted on Vimeo, and&nbsp;
still viewable on Hunter's personal website.

00:02:27.138 --> 00:02:30.960
For Melissa Hunter and all involved, the&nbsp;
abrupt end of "Adult Wednesday Addams" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.380
devastating. To make matters worse, even&nbsp;
when she tried to keep her series alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.500
in the face of imminent cancellation,&nbsp;
the parties involved simply couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.460
make a deal happen. In a Twitter post&nbsp;
from February of 2021, Hunter wrote,

00:02:49.131 --> 00:02:52.980
Hunter's tweet, of course, was in response to&nbsp;
the Netflix Twitter account announcing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.700
new series, "Wednesday." However, Hunter&nbsp;
also made it clear that she wasn't angry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.200
and was instead happy that Wednesday maintains&nbsp;
a place of pop culture prominence. She tweeted,

00:03:07.098 --> 00:03:10.800
There are many reasons to be excited about the&nbsp;
Netflix premiere of "Wednesday." But if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.240
hoping for new episodes of Melissa Hunter's "Adult&nbsp;
Wednesday Addams," you're probably out of luck.

